WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.699
These days if you want to simulate something
in the physical world

00:00:02.699 --> 00:00:03.790
you use a computer.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:06.050
But what if you couldn't?

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:07.220
What if it was, say, the 1950s

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.030
and you needed to work out if a bold but questionable
plan

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:14.329
to dam the San Francisco Bay was a good idea?

00:00:14.329 --> 00:00:19.700
The answer is this: The US Army Corps of Engineers
Bay Model.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.570
The Bay Model is one and a half acres or more.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:27.120
What you're looking at is one of our former
scientific, hydrodynamic,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.930
engineering testing facilities.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:33.381
And this was the tool, the instrument, that
they used to see

00:00:33.381 --> 00:00:38.230
what the unforeseen consequences of the John
Reber Plan was going to be.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:41.470
In the 1940s and 50s a man named John Reber

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:45.280
had a plan to completely change the San Francisco
Bay.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.450
Enormous dams would create freshwater lakes.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:52.110
There'd be brand-new reclaimed land for industry
and for air and naval bases.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:56.120
Reber said it would make the bay a defensible
military fortification,

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.770
move people safely inland

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:02.290
and the newly dammed rivers would provide
huge amounts of drinking water.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.100
Reber was not a professional engineer.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.450
He was a theatrical producer who had done
a lot of research,

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:11.110
but because he worked in showbiz, he knew
how to promote something.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:15.700
And by most accounts he was a friendly, sincere,
convincing man.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:21.490
So unlike other ideas for giant engineering
projects, the Reber Plan actually caught on.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.049
Debate went back and forth for years but eventually

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:27.180
Reber's plan seemed realistic enough, at least
to politicians,

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.610
that the US Army Corps of Engineers were tasked
to see if it was practical

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:37.340
and they were given $2.5m, that's about $25m
today, to find out.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.380
And with it, they built this.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.619
This model when it was built was the next
level, the next generation.

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:46.780
It was extremely accurate as an instrument.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.060
At the time there really wasn't anything better.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:54.299
There's 250,000 strategically placed little
copper tabs in the bottom of the model

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:58.119
to keep the saltwater and the freshwater from
going in and out too quickly,

00:01:58.119 --> 00:02:04.009
but also to duplicate any little protrusions
sticking up out of the bottom of the bay.

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:06.560
The model is 1:1000 scale horizontally,

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.880
1:100 scale vertically and 1:100 scale in
time.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.970
That means about two hundred times a day,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.140
the tide comes in and the tide goes out,

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.180
because San Francisco Bay has tides and therefore,
so does the model.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.980
Everything was hand operated for the first
thirty years.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:27.060
It took probably anywhere between twelve and
fifteen people to operate it,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.670
sometimes it was as many as sixty people here
at one time

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.940
working on various different experiments

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.890
and everybody had to be totally in sync.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.090
It was like an orchestra.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:41.020
And the interns were out there in chairs,
in lab coats, in the water

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.260
measuring the various different ebb and flow
of the tide.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.830
It took three years for Reber's plan to be
tested here

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.030
and in that time Reber passed away.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:54.379
And for his supporters, the results from the
model were devastating.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:58.110
It looked great on paper, convinced a lot
of people,

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:02.570
but when it was tested they found that it
was only good on paper.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.600
The end result was it failed on ninety-nine
different accounts.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.580
Wild and unpredictable catastrophic flooding
was just one of them.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.730
The dams wouldn't create lakes, they'd create
evaporation ponds.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.890
The tide would create dangerous currents and
waves.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:20.440
In short, not only would the Reber Plan have
been a disaster,

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.270
it would have been a billion dollar disaster.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.150
The ecosystem would have been devastated too,

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.040
but it was the 50s, so no-one was really thinking
about that.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.810
The Corps of Engineers, their job done,

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.239
figured the model would come in useful again
someday.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:34.239
And it did,

00:03:34.239 --> 00:03:38.430
helping to test smaller and more practical
schemes across the bay for decades.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.200
These days, of course, computers can do all
of that

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.349
for a fraction of the time and the money,

00:03:42.349 --> 00:03:44.730
but it's still a good educational resource.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.349
Last time I talked about a grand civil engineering
scheme like this,

00:03:47.349 --> 00:03:48.840
about Herman Sörgel's Atlantropa,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.780
I said it was a testament to how big we can
dream,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.330
but this model is a testament to something
else.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.330
To science.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:56.910
To having a hypothesis.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.070
To testing it.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.270
And then, when it fails, admitting that it's
wrong.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.220
There shouldn't be any shame in that.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:05.410
Sometimes we follow bad ideas,

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.400
and changing your mind based on new evidence

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.750
and allowing others to do the same

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.549
is something our world should be built on,

00:04:11.549 --> 00:04:13.860
and it's exactly what this model made happen.

